# Cross-Repo Dependency Resolution Reference

## Overview

A repo like `falcon` (API gateway, no DB of its own) cannot be bootstrapped in
isolation — the entities it serves live in upstream services such as `eyas` or
`yarak`. The skill must locate those upstream repos on disk to know where to
read schemas, run migrations, and seed data. This reference documents how the
skill detects upstreams from source signals, resolves service names to local
repo paths, and handles cases where no local path exists (remote-only or SaaS
services).

---

## Detection Signals

A positive hit from any command below is a candidate upstream. Multiple signals
for the same name increase confidence; a single signal still requires user
confirmation before the skill treats the upstream as authoritative.

| Signal | What it tells us |
|---|---|
| `docker-compose.yml` service entries | Sibling services that may run alongside the target |
| `.env*` / `application.yml` URL env vars | External services the app calls at runtime |
| gRPC client stubs / generated proto code | Services with proto-defined interfaces |
| Imported clients (e.g. `EyasClient`, `YarakSDK`) | Named upstreams referenced in source |
| `package.json` workspace refs | Monorepo siblings already on disk |

### docker-compose.yml service entries

```bash
# Preferred — list service names via yq
yq '.services | keys' docker-compose.yml 2>/dev/null

# Fallback — grep top-level service keys
grep -E '^  [a-zA-Z0-9_-]+:' docker-compose.yml 2>/dev/null | head -20
```

Application services with a `build:` stanza are likely local repos. Services
using official images (`postgres`, `redis`, `mongo`) are infrastructure — record
them for storage-kind detection but do not search for repos.

### URL env vars in .env files and application config

```bash
# .env* files — extract upstream service URLs
grep -hE '^[A-Z_]+_URL=' .env* 2>/dev/null

# application.yml / application.yaml — service URLs
grep -rn --include="application.yml" --include="application.yaml" \
  -E '(url:|baseUrl:|serviceUrl:|endpoint:)' \
  --exclude-dir=target --exclude-dir=build \
  . 2>/dev/null | head -10

# .NET appsettings — service URIs
grep -rn --include="appsettings*.json" \
  -E '"(ServiceUrl|BaseUrl|Endpoint)"' \
  . 2>/dev/null | head -10
```

Each hostname or service name in a URL (e.g., `http://eyas-service:8080`) is a
candidate upstream. Strip the scheme, port, and path to get the service name,
then apply naming normalization (see below).

### gRPC generated stubs

```bash
# Python gRPC stubs
find . -not -path "*/node_modules/*" -not -path "*/target/*" -not -path "*/build/*" \
  -not -path "*/vendor/*" -not -path "*/.git/*" \
  -name '*_pb2_grpc.py' \
  2>/dev/null | head -10

# Java / Kotlin gRPC stubs
find . -not -path "*/node_modules/*" -not -path "*/target/*" -not -path "*/build/*" \
  -not -path "*/vendor/*" -not -path "*/.git/*" \
  \( -name '*ClientStub.kt' -o -name '*ClientStub.java' \) \
  2>/dev/null | head -10

# Go gRPC generated files
find . -not -path "*/build/*" -not -path "*/vendor/*" -not -path "*/.git/*" \
  -name '*_grpc.pb.go' \
  2>/dev/null | head -10
```

Extract the service name from the stub filename or the package declaration at
the top of each file. Generated stubs that name services not present in
docker-compose or env config may be phantom services — reconcile before treating
them as authoritative (see Edge cases).

### Imported clients (Java / Kotlin / TypeScript)

```bash
# Java / Kotlin — named client imports
grep -rn --include='*.java' --include='*.kt' \
  --exclude-dir=target --exclude-dir=build \
  -E 'import .*Client\b' . 2>/dev/null | head -10

# TypeScript / JavaScript — SDK imports
grep -rn --include='*.ts' --include='*.js' \
  --exclude-dir=node_modules --exclude-dir=dist \
  -E "from\s+['\"].*[Cc]lient['\"]|require\(['\"].*[Cc]lient['\"]" \
  . 2>/dev/null | head -10
```

Client class names like `EyasClient` or `YarakSDK` directly name the upstream.
Strip the `Client` / `SDK` suffix, apply normalization, and record as a
candidate.

### package.json workspace refs

```bash
# Root-level workspaces array (Yarn / npm workspaces)
jq '.workspaces // []' package.json 2>/dev/null

# Lerna config
cat lerna.json 2>/dev/null | jq '.packages // []'

# pnpm workspaces
cat pnpm-workspace.yaml 2>/dev/null
```

Workspaces are already on disk — no sibling-directory search needed. Treat each
workspace path as a resolved local repo path.

